# ChatterBox – Real-Time Messaging Application

A real-time chat application built with **NestJS** and **Socket.IO**.  
It enables instant messaging between connected users, online presence tracking, and the ability for users to delete their own messages.

---

## Features

- Real-time bidirectional communication using WebSockets
- Online user presence tracking
- System messages when users join or leave
- Users can delete only their own messages
- Event-driven architecture
- Clean implementation using NestJS WebSocket Gateway
- CORS enabled for easy frontend connection

---

## Tech Stack

| Category       | Technology                                      |
|----------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| Framework      | NestJS                                          |
| Real-time      | Socket.IO (`@nestjs/websockets`, `@nestjs/platform-socket.io`) |
| Language       | TypeScript                                      |
| Documentation  | Swagger (optional)                              |

---

## How It Works

The core logic lives inside the WebSocket Gateway:

1. Clients connect to the Socket.IO server
2. Users join the chat by sending a `join` event with their username
3. Messages are broadcast to all connected clients in real time
4. The list of online users is updated whenever someone joins or leaves
5. Users can emit a delete event to remove their own messages

---

## Project Structure

src/
├── gateway/
│   └── web-socket.gateway.ts     # Main Socket.IO Gateway
├── controller/
│   └── web-socket.ts
├── module/
│   └── web-socket.module.ts
├── app.module.ts
└── main.ts

---

## Getting Started

### Prerequisites

- Node.js v18 or higher
- Git

### Installation

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/TechieJoe/chatterBox.git

# Navigate into the project
cd chatterBox

# Install dependencies
npm install

# Running the Application

# Development mode
npm run start:dev

# Production mode
npm run build

The server will start on the default port (usually 3000).
You can connect any Socket.IO client or frontend application to the WebSocket endpoint.

# Main Socket Events

Event           Direction           Description
join           Client → Server    User joins the chat with a username
sendMessage    Client → Server     Send a chat message
systemMessage  Server → Client     System notifications (join/leave)
userList       Server → Client      Updated list of currently online users
deleteMessage  Client → Server    Delete a message owned by the user
npm run start:prod

# Example Client Connection (JavaScript)

const socket = io('http://localhost:3000');

// Join the chat
socket.emit('join', 'JohnDoe');

// Send a message
socket.emit('sendMessage', {
  id: Date.now().toString(),
  message: 'Hello everyone!'
});

// Listen for messages
socket.on('systemMessage', (msg) => {
  console.log('System:', msg);
});   

